# Declarative diagnostic and documentation catalog.
# Run perl regen/diag.pl after editing this file.
# Regeneration normalizes the data below and preserves this POD.

use utf8;

=pod

=head1 NAME

diag_conf.pl - adding or changing a Perl core diagnostic

=head1 ADD OR CHANGE A MESSAGE

Search for an existing message first. Reuse its code if the message and
arguments have the same meaning. Otherwise, copy a similar definition under
C<diagnostics-E<gt>{severity}{kind}{code}> and give it a new code and
number.
Choose the next number after the highest active or retired number. Numbers
in C<retired_numbers> cannot be reused. For example:

    FATAL_ERROR => {
        CORE_RUNTIME => {
            INVALID_WIDGET => {
                number   => 2437,
                severity => 'FATAL_ERROR',
                kind     => 'CORE_RUNTIME',
                code     => 'INVALID_WIDGET',
                message => {
                    templates => { en => 'Invalid widget %{name}' },
                    args => [
                        {
                            name => 'name', type => 'text',
                            format_str => 's',
                        },
                    ],
                },
                emit => { api => 'CROAK' },
            },
        },
    },

The severity, kind, and code must match the enclosing keys. Keep existing
codes, numbers, and macro argument order when changing a message.
Codes and numbers must be unique across all diagnostic groups. Register a
new kind in C<exception_kinds> with its own unique code and number.
Use C<CORE_RUNTIME> for runtime errors and C<CORE_COMPILE> for parser errors.
Choose a severity from the classifications in L<perldiag>: C<WARNING>,
C<DEPRECATION>, C<SEVERE_WARNING>, C<FATAL_ERROR>, C<INTERNAL_ERROR>,
C<UNTRAPPABLE_ERROR>, or C<ALIEN_ERROR>.

=head1 SOURCE REFERENCES

C<source_files> is a derived, sorted list of repository-relative filenames
where a diagnostic is referenced. Regeneration rebuilds the list from
MANIFEST-listed core and extension C, C++, header, XS, and grammar sources.
It excludes generated catalog output, the duplicate F<ext/re> sources,
vendored C<cpan> and C<dist> sources, and Perl-level tests and libraries.

The scan recognizes generated emitter and formatter macros, aliases, fixed
variants, symbolic diagnostic indices/numbers, and exact quoted diagnostic
codes. It ignores comments. Extension C/XS test fixtures are included.
A reference can be an emitter, a forwarding API, or a framework check, so
this list does not prove where a diagnostic is emitted. An empty list does
not prove the entry is unused. Line numbers are omitted to avoid churn.
Do not maintain these lists by hand.

=head1 CLASSIFICATION TAGS

C<tags> is an optional list of overlapping classification tags on a
diagnostic. The shared C<diagnostic_tags> metadata defines their names and
descriptions, and records whether the assignment set is provisional or
reviewed. For example:

    tags => [ 'subject:object_role_field',
              'condition:undefined_uninitialized' ],

The initial set comes from the classification audit. It contains code-name
signals and selected producer checks, and is marked provisional. Tags do
not select a diagnostic class, template, severity, or delivery API.

Regeneration preserves and sorts the lists. It rejects unknown tags,
duplicate tags, and malformed lists or shared definitions. Review the
producer before treating an inferred tag as a semantic guarantee.

=head1 PANIC TRACKING

C<is_panic> records a panic classification for catalog bookkeeping. It does
not select a runtime class or change delivery. A stored C<0> or C<1> is an
explicit decision and is preserved.

When the field is absent, regeneration searches the diagnostic code, macro
name, description, and templates in every language for the word C<panic>,
ignoring case. Underscores delimit the word in identifiers. A match adds
C<is_panic =E<gt> 1>. No match leaves the field absent so a later edit can be
detected. Remove a stored value to request autodetection again.

=head1 RENDER-ONLY FORMATS

C<is_render =E<gt> 1> identifies a formatting-only entry. Regeneration derives
this flag from C<emit.api =E<gt> 'RENDER'> and rejects a conflicting value.
Its C<class> records the subject or contextual role, using the same hierarchy
as ordinary diagnostics. There is no separate Render class family.

The class is classification metadata. The generated format macro does not
construct an instance, change severity handling, or invoke delivery hooks.
Warning classes assigned to formats do not change their callers' warning
categories or enablement rules.

=head1 UNICODE TEXT

This file is UTF-8 source and declares C<use utf8;>. Write printable Unicode
text directly in catalog strings. Regeneration uses Data::Dumper's C<Useutf8>
option when available and keeps controls, separators, default-ignorable
characters, and other non-printing characters escaped. Its compatibility
path supports older installed Data::Dumper versions without changing the
catalog's character data.

Write C<%{name}> for a message argument and C<%%> for a literal percent sign.
Arguments have three roles, which may overlap:

=over 4

=item *

Input arguments are the values accepted by the generated macro.

=item *

Selector arguments choose a template case.

=item *

Template arguments provide values for C<%{name}> placeholders.

=back

An input may be transformed or combined to produce a named argument. That
argument may be used both as a selector and in templates. A selector-only
argument does not need to appear in a template. Each template may use zero or
more template arguments. Every placeholder used by any template must have an
argument definition, but an argument need not appear in every template. The
generated display-parameter layout is the union of the placeholders used
across all languages and cases.

Describe each named argument in C<message.args>. A printf formatter omits its
leading percent: use C<s> for C<%s>, C<d> for C<%d>, and so on. For an SV, use:

    { name => 'name', type => 'text',
      format_str => 'SVf', pass => 'SVfARG' }

Copy an existing parameter definition for formats that take several inputs,
such as C<UTF8f> or C<%.*s>. To combine several macro arguments into one
diagnostic parameter, use C<pass =E<gt> 'sv_setpvf'>, put the complete
format in C<format_str>, and list its inputs in order. C<sv_catpvf> can be
used when the generated code should append formatted text. For example,
C<%s%.*s> combines a sigil, a byte length, and a variable name. An optional
C<utf8_input> names an integer input whose true value marks the combined
string as UTF-8. Preserve the original formatting and delivery
when replacing an existing error call. C<emit.api> chooses delivery:
C<CROAK> for an ordinary exception, C<YYERROR> for a collected parser error,
or the original warning API for a warning. Follow a matching definition
when encoding flags, warning categories, or other API inputs are needed. Put
inputs required by the delivery API in C<emit.args>. Set C<emit.expression>
when the generated macro must remain usable inside another C expression.
Set C<emit.accumulate_macro> on a warning when a compile-time parser call site
needs to append it to the active exception instead of delivering it
immediately. The ordinary warning macro retains immediate delivery for
runtime call sites.

C<RENDER> declares a catalog format used only during rendering. It generates
C<PERL_FORMAT_CODE(language, values)>, which returns a mortal formatted SV.
It does not construct a diagnostic or invoke delivery hooks. C<values> is
an AV containing already formatted display values followed by selector-only
values, in catalog metadata order. Render formats share template validation,
translation import/export, selector matching, and English fallback with
ordinary diagnostics. Their numeric identities do not represent additional
records in the exception.

Use C<message.selectors> when semantic values choose different complete
messages. List selector objects in lookup order. Each has a C<name> that
references an argument in C<message.args> and a C<values> list. A later
selector may use a nested C<values> hash keyed by earlier selector values when
its valid values depend on them. Store templates as nested hashes in the same
order under C<message.templates>. A C<'*'> or C<any> branch is a fallback for
values at that level. Normalization stores C<*>. Defining both spellings in
one branch is an error. Every declared combination must resolve to a complete
message, possibly through English fallback.
Each leaf uses only the template arguments relevant to that message. The
overall display-parameter layout is shared across all languages and cases.
Put C<selector_values> on an argument when its value selects the template.
That argument may also appear as a rendered argument in the template. Use a
separate selector definition only when the selector key is derived from a
different argument.

An explicit selector may set C<wide =E<gt> 1> to use a U16 value field. At
most one selector per diagnostic may be wide. It always occupies packed key
positions 7 and 8; up to six narrow selectors occupy positions 1 through 6
in their configuration order. Narrow-only diagnostics retain up to seven
U8 selectors. Zero remains the wildcard for either field width.

Normalization sorts selector value arrays alphabetically, including array
leaves in conditional domains. Set C<selector_unsorted =E<gt> 1> on an argument
to preserve its configured C<selector_values> order, or C<unsorted =E<gt> 1>
on an explicit selector definition. A marked use preserves the order of the
shared array for every alias. The shared C<reftype_name> domain is marked so
normalization retains its reference-type ordering.

Values receive IDs starting at one in their configured order; zero means
wildcard. The shared C<reftype_name> domain deliberately follows native
C<SVrt_*> order rather than alphabetical order, with C<UNKNOWN> first.
Its selector IDs are one greater than the native IDs: C<UNKNOWN> is one
(native zero), C<ARRAY> is two, C<HASH> is three, and so on.

An emitter may define C<format_macro> to retain a legacy format-string
interface. Normally its value is derived from the English template. If that
format string must stay stable for compatibility or dispatch, set
C<emit.format_macro_format> to its legacy printf format. This format is not a
diagnostic template and must not be used to build the structured message.

A C<RENDER> entry without selectors may expose C<format_macro> for constrained
output before exception delivery is available, or where allocation is unsafe.
This compile-time English format does not allocate or invoke hooks. Ordinary
runtime reporting should use structured diagnostic delivery instead.

C<emit.format_macro_chunks> on such a C<RENDER> entry also generates
C<FORMAT_NAME_TEXT_0>, C<FORMAT_NAME_TEXT_1>, and so on: the literal text
before, between, and after its named placeholders. Percent escapes are
decoded for direct writing. This is for allocation-free fallback writers
that already supply values in template order, such as allocator assertions.
The complete English template owns the grammar; C code supplies only values.

For C<YYERROR>, C<YYERROR_PV>, and C<YYERROR_PVN> delivery, declare
C<emit.parser_action>. C<collect> reports the message and lets parsing proceed;
the existing ten-error limit can still stop compilation. C<recover> reports
the message and enters grammar recovery through C<YYERROR>. C<abort> reports
the message and calls C<yyquit()> to stop immediately. Use C<recover> only
for grammar actions. Opaque text adapters whose nullable input decides
whether to report or abort use C<caller> until their producers are resolved.
When the same message needs different actions, add action-specific variants
with empty C<values> and an C<emit.parser_action> override. Keep each action at
its own symbolic call site.

=head1 LANGUAGE RULES

C<plural_rules> owns shared integer C expressions and their standard forms.
Each language names its
C<cardinal> and C<ordinal> rule under C<languages>. For example:

    plural_rules => {
        cardinal_rule_1 => {
            forms => [qw(one other)],
            expression => '(n) == 1 ? "one" : "other"',
        },
        ordinal_rule_1 => {
            forms => ['other'], expression => '"other"',
        },
    },
    languages => {
        de => { cardinal => 'cardinal_rule_1',
                ordinal  => 'ordinal_rule_1' },
        nl => { cardinal => 'cardinal_rule_1',
                ordinal  => 'ordinal_rule_1' },
    },

Both rule references are required for each catalog language. Rule names start
with C<cardinal_> or C<ordinal_>; a reference must use the correct kind. Each
expression uses C<n> and returns one of C<"zero">, C<"one">, C<"two">,
C<"few">, C<"many">, or C<"other">. Regeneration validates rule definitions,
category names, missing references, and rule kinds before writing output.
The C<forms> declaration must match the categories named by the expression.
It contains no exact numeric cases and no wildcard.
There are no language-to-language alias chains.

The current expressions reference CLDR 48 integer rules. They do not handle
fractions or compact-number operands.

The generator emits per-language rule macros and combined dispatch macros
within the generated diagnostic catalogs. These rules belong to each
diagnostic engine. The C<cardinal_plural_form> and C<ordinal_plural_form>
queries instead use the complete retained CLDR integer-rule table, compiled
through F<regen/diag_plural.pm> into F<perldiag_plural.inc>. Those queries do
not depend on loaded diagnostic engines. Their C API bodies stay in
F<pp_ctl.c> and remain registered in F<embed.fnc>.

Exact-number config keys are bare strings such as C<"0"> and C<"21">,
without C<=>. Exact-number overrides and wildcard matching belong to template
selection, not to the plural rule expressions. C<other> remains a category,
separate from the C<*> wildcard.

A plural selector names its unsigned integer source and rule kind instead
of declaring a C<values> list:

    selectors => [
        { name => 'quantity', source => 'n', plural => 'cardinal' },
        { name => 'rank', source => 'position', plural => 'ordinal' },
    ],

The source arguments use C<type =E<gt> 'uv'> and C<format_str =E<gt> 'UVuf'>.
They remain in the diagnostic even when no template displays them. Each
selector's shared domain contains the six standard categories and the union
of bare exact-number keys used at its depth in every language. The wildcard
remains distinct from that domain. Do not enumerate a Cartesian product.

Each diagnostic, selector, and language has a generated resolver which tests
that language's exact counts first, then applies its cardinal or ordinal
rule. Exact cases from another language extend the shared domain without
overriding this language's category. Standard categories have fixed enum IDs,
followed by exact numeric cases in ascending numeric order. Existing
combined-selector precedence applies: more populated octets first, then
ascending packed key. English fallback repeats resolution with English
rules and exact cases. Multiple plural selectors can use independent counts
and rule kinds in the same template.

=head1 USE THE MESSAGE

Replace the error call with the generated macro and its arguments:

    PERL_THROW_INVALID_WIDGET(name);

Use C<PERL_THROW_> for fatal errors, C<PERL_PANIC_> for internal errors,
C<PERL_WARN_> for warnings, C<PERL_DEPRECATE_> for deprecations,
C<PERL_ADD_COMPILE_ERR_> for collected compilation errors, and
C<PERL_CHECK_TAINT_> for taint checks, followed by the diagnostic code.
Regeneration records the name in C<macro>. Set that field explicitly if a
different name is needed. Macro names must be unique.
Pass arguments in C<message.args> order, followed by any C<emit.args> inputs.
Keep the message text and formatting in this file.

When messages share a template, put their fixed values in C<variants>:

    variants => {
        INVALID_INPUT_WIDGET => {
            values => { name => { en => 'input' } },
        },
    },

This generates C<PERL_THROW_INVALID_INPUT_WIDGET()> as a wrapper around
C<PERL_THROW_INVALID_WIDGET("input")>. Unbound parameters remain macro
arguments. Keep fixed wording in the catalog and use the per-message macro
at the call site. Fixed text uses language keys under C<message.templates>.
The generator currently emits English. Translations remain in the catalog.
Use C<translation_review> for language-specific follow-up notes when a
translation depends on message selection or argument processing that the
current template format cannot express. This metadata does not affect runtime
diagnostics.

Declare a delivery alias as an object in C<emit.aliases> with its API and,
when needed, its macro name:

    emit => {
        api => 'CROAK',
        aliases => [
            { api => 'DIE', macro => 'PERL_THROW_INVALID_WIDGET_DIE' },
        ],
    }

Use C<emit =E<gt> { api =E<gt> 'DIE' }> on a variant when its caller needs a
different delivery declared in the definition's C<emit.aliases>. Each variant
has its own unique code and macro name. Regeneration records the macro name
and preserves explicit names. Variants can bind simple C<text> arguments
with C<format_str =E<gt> 's'>. Keep other formatting contracts distinct.
Omit C<emit> on a variant that uses its parent's delivery API. Put a variant's
parser action in C<emit.parser_action>.
If a definition is removed, add its number to C<retired_numbers>.

Add or update the explanation in C<documentation.entries>. Follow a nearby
entry's C<code>, C<headings>, and C<text> layout. Its code identifies the
explanation and is separate from the diagnostic code. Do not edit the
generated F<pod/perldiag.pod> or F<perldiag.h>.

=head1 CHECK THE CHANGE

Run regeneration with the environment Perl:

    perl regen/diag.pl
    perl regen/diag.pl --tap
    ./perl -Ilib t/porting/diag_migration.t
    ./perl -Ilib t/porting/diag.t

To generate a readable translation worksheet for one language, set
C<MAKE_TRANS_TABLE> while running the normal regeneration target:

    MAKE_TRANS_TABLE=fr make regen

This writes C<perldiag_trans_fr.json>. Each entry includes the stable number,
symbolic code, selector values, English source, and the French translation.
Missing translations contain the English text and have C<translated> set to
C<false>. The JSON includes instructions for editing only the translation
field while preserving argument placeholders and selector values.

Rebuild Perl and run tests for the affected behavior. Regeneration sorts and
formats the catalog.

=cut

