      {
        "code" => "PERLDIAG_1198",
        "headings" => [
          "%s \"\\x%X\" does not map to Unicode\n\n"
        ],
        "text" => "(S utf8) When reading in different encodings, Perl tries to\nmap everything into Unicode characters.  The bytes you read\nin are not legal in this encoding.  For example\n\n    utf8 \"\\xE4\" does not map to Unicode\n\nif you try to read in the a-diaereses Latin-1 as UTF-8.\n\n"
      },
      {
        "code" => "PERLDIAG_1199",
        "headings" => [
          "\\x{%X} is a \\w char that isn't valid in a name \"%s\"\n\n"
        ],
        "text" => "(F) There are fewer than 200 characters in Unicode that you might expect\nto be able to appear in an identifier or other name, but are actually\nillegal.  Perl encountered one of them when parsing the name given by\nthe C<%s>.  Its Unicode code point is shown.  You'll need to choose a\ndifferent name.\n\nMost of the illegal ones involve a character enclosed in some shape,\nsuch as CIRCLED LATIN CAPITAL LETTER M.  But others are because of\nproblematic semantics.  MIDDLE DOT, for example, is punctuation in some\nlanguages, but is a word letter in others.  You can generate a complete\nlist of the current ones by running this program\n\n perl -le 'use re qw(Debug COMPILE); qr/(?[ \\w ^ \\p{XIDC} ])/'\n\nLook near the final line.  The one that begins C<stclass> contains a\nlist of 4 and 5 digit hexdecimal numbers.  These are the Unicode code\npoints that are illegal in names.  You can use\nL<charnames::viacode|charnames/charnames::viacode(code)> or many\ninternet search engines to look up their names if desired.\n\n"
      },
